# Real findings that are not secrets, and why.
#
# Every one has been read. None of them opens anything that exists: they are
# the password of a database that is born and dies with `docker compose up` on
# the reader's own machine, the four demonstration accounts, and two variables
# that read a value rather than hold one.
#
# The demonstration accounts are in the code AND in the README on purpose. This
# project demonstrates a boundary of permissions, and a boundary you cannot
# stand on both sides of is an assertion rather than a demonstration: the four
# identities are there so the difference can be seen in one click. The database
# they open is created empty on the reader's machine and thrown away with the
# container.
#
# It does not apply to company names or to real keys. Those block from any
# file, for any reason.
#
# A note for whoever writes here: a reason must not reproduce the code it
# excuses. This file is examined like every other one, and a reason that quotes
# the offending line reports itself.

docker-compose.yml                       # the throwaway PostgreSQL password of the demonstration
.github/workflows/ci.yml                 # the same one, for the CI's PostgreSQL service
backend/db/pools.js                      # reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD from the environment, with a default for the demo
backend/auth/routes.js                   # a variable that receives what the client sent, not a value written here
web/src/app/shell/api.service.ts         # the signature of the method that sends the credentials to the API
backend/auth/passwords.js                # the sentence about a minimum length
backend/seed/demo.js                     # the four demonstration accounts, documented in the README
web/src/app/auth/sign-in.component.ts    # the same, as buttons on the sign-in page
tools/walkthrough.mjs                    # the same, to drive the running system
README.md                                # the table that lists them, which is half the demonstration
tools/through-the-screen.mjs             # the same, to drive the path through the interface
tools/who-can-do-what.mjs                # the same, to try every role against what it is promised
tools/screenshots.mjs                    # the same, to remake the README's figures as each of them
